{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-37712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-1y \\\\ -10x+2y\\end{aligned}]$\nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -48\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ -84\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-37712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${v}_{2}$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}6x-4y \\\\ 2x-y \\\\ -5x+3y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{i})\\ne 0$ for any $i$'s, we conclude that none of them belongs to the kernel of $\\,T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-40179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{1}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40179" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-139998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y \\\\ 6x+3y\\end{aligned}]$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-139998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is ${v}_{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-139998" "