"file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the vectors that belong to the kernel of $T$ are ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-81386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ only"] [