\\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-37840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "None of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${v}_{3}$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z \\\\ -6x+3y-3z \\\\ 4x-2y+2z\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 3-1+k \\\\ -6\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 1-3\\cdot k \\\\ 4\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5+k \\\\ -15-3k \\\\ 10+2k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5+k=0 \\\\ -15-3k=0 \\\\ 10+2k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-81388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-140011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+4y \\\\ 5x+10y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-140011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot k+4\\cdot 3 \\\\ 5\\cdot k+10\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2k+12 \\\\ 5k+30\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2k+12=0 \\\\ 5k+30=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=-6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-140011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-140011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-140011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "12"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-140011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x-2y \\\\ -10x+5y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot k-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ -10\\cdot k+5\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4k-12 \\\\ -10k+30\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4k-12=0 \\\\ -10k+30=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37825" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-37825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37825" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3x+6y \\\\ 2x-4y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot k+6\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2\\cdot k-4\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3k+12 \\\\ 2k-8\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3k+12=0 \\\\ 2k-8=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-81387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-8x+4y \\\\ 6x-3y \\\\ 2x-y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-8\\cdot 3+4\\cdot k \\\\ 6\\cdot 3-3\\cdot k \\\\ 2\\cdot 3-k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-24+4k \\\\ 18-3k \\\\ 6-k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-24+4k=0 \\\\ 18-3k=0 \\\\ 6-k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-37842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z \\\\ -12x-4y+4z \\\\ 6x+8y+10z\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-2z.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9x-3y+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x-3(-2z)+3z & =0 \\\\ -9x+9z & =0 \\\\ x & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37827" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-37827" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37827" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ x-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,z\\in R}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-3y\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,y\\in R}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37827" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,z\\in R}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-38719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-4y+2z \\\\ x-3y+z \\\\ 4x-4y+z\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-38719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x,$ $y,$ and $z.$ From the third equation, we obtain $z=0$ and from the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we have $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-38719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-38719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-38719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}-y \\\\ y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y\\in R}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,z\\in R}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-38719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-6x+y \\\\ 12x-2y\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{6}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]y,\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-40175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-140052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4x-2y \\\\ -6x+3y\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-140052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{6}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}y.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=y[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-140052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-140052" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-140052" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-140052" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y-6z-6x \\\\ -4y+x+z \\\\ 2y+8z+8x\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{12}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{7}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have $x=-z.$ \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]z,\\,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-37828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-37828" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x-8y \\\\ 16y-6x\\end{aligned}],$\nfind  $2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $2$nd one). \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)-3=2\\cdot 1-3=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-81391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-z \\\\ x-y+z \\\\ 3z-6x\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -6 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $3$rd one). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-81392" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81392" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-81392" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-80723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y+3z \\\\ 8x-3y+10z \\\\ -6x+3y-9z\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-80723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot columns (the third one). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-80723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-80723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-80723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-80723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3x-6y \\\\ 2x-4y\\end{aligned}],$ find $9\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot column. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=0.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n9\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+4=9\\cdot 0+4=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-40177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "22"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-40177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-38676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x-7y \\\\ 14y-10x\\end{aligned}],$\nfind  $3\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-38676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -10 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $1$ non-pivot column (the $2$nd one). \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=1.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n3\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+1=3\\cdot 1+1=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-38676" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a579064a7cfdf12bbe0566432048615661cc343b7b4be0d02239938278a1a2a"} {"question" "q-38676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-38676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "q-38676" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/state.json" "file_sha256" "46b1b4ee500d9566609f05fbfb2e9451fcd9e333738d123bb3040e5dcab962db"} {"question" "e-5616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/c3d64ddcfd33db2e96caf6db5d85587857c3715e8d91a7dc8f4801c117fc89b0.png) \nand the vectors \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/4fbbb82838d7fa94fe0aeb47ed48952bfe573e12874d3891f8650a15b46e0ee7.png)\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/example-5616.json" "file_sha256" "3b4f2512628e39d087bdddefb3add655c8480af533628e8026e3dce63159be21"} {"question" "e-5616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0},$ we conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/example-5616.json" "file_sha256" "3b4f2512628e39d087bdddefb3add655c8480af533628e8026e3dce63159be21"} {"question" "e-5441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/d0a0fabd7275cdceed8c0c62e58bbfc0e5f23322145d551e412877075cbb6e3c.png) and the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/4dd5f4c300c5870e696c5485fffdef1bbd9b7741b23c7d27fda1198e31baa33b.png) Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/example-5441.json" "file_sha256" "05c69eb6d107d3e9b2b98d440c518af7e99288d2be7883f1dd0020debdc36d6e"} {"question" "e-5441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot k+12\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 6 \\\\ -2\\cdot k+4\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ 6\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ -2k-4 \\\\ 6k+12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ -2k-4=0 \\\\ 6k+12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/example-5441.json" "file_sha256" "05c69eb6d107d3e9b2b98d440c518af7e99288d2be7883f1dd0020debdc36d6e"} {"question" "e-5439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/3d11ab5d1681b0aefa6c64c4f6dad9cdf396f61d00ac0b90cb1efa64881c54cc.png) Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/example-5439.json" "file_sha256" "4c48e463c8a8a64af38208dda214a949062349c375e2e602fd22027442d675aa"} {"question" "e-5439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Finding the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/5fa2e7ff6cfc27fce7698124fb835abf5c6f02221ba7c7ebb0b371fef6d3c15a.png)\n\nSetting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/f9fc80048a348773c656ec98ac9299eb1c611bfb07eee747b883eb7398dc295f.png) we then need to solve the equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/cace77f13c7ef817cfefaab742bdca008b29a177b0783dc761bc24d8a1273af3.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/example-5439.json" "file_sha256" "4c48e463c8a8a64af38208dda214a949062349c375e2e602fd22027442d675aa"} {"question" "e-5440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the linear transformation ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/a441d65e864253e32c59c58b6b0684482b6eaee7fd443fdb2f7c026f0fe10d1f.png) find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/example-5440.json" "file_sha256" "35d84840ac55fa537d947bc5d53fc9c43c54ffdc204b802e9019c73d3f2b26bc"} {"question" "e-5440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/assets/f27d3195556ff8786f9416cddbe4ad52cf1838f2b0ce37b4da11746fee59fb9c.png)\n\nRecall that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/example-5440.json" "file_sha256" "35d84840ac55fa537d947bc5d53fc9c43c54ffdc204b802e9019c73d3f2b26bc"} {"question" "q-38719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "4c76565f6732780519e7e7169fb8dd76e59e0c1fc2b7f627cb2db0b162cd8841" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "For the original transformation, solving T(x,y,z)=0 gives equations 2x-4y+2z=0, x-3y+z=0, and 4x-4y+z=0. Subtracting twice the second equation from the first gives 2y=0. Subtracting four times the second equation from the third gives 8y-3z=0, hence z=0 and then x=0. Therefore Ker(T)={(0,0,0)}, original option b (index 1). This derived judgment agrees with the authentic worked solution and saved Correct grading. The stored bmatrix and captured bracketed aligned vector preserve the same singleton set and ordered entries." "bindings" {"problem" "57e37a56d67e7134705a6a335aee9c3109ad7033dff00df4ee2431084ac0f7c8" "worked_solution" "c194ca19f8829c6f1238bafec58ca1fdef6335a5a49d4c30835d4a6b9e3fe183" "original_field" "f7a14cfd44aa0fb7b6dcf9237c89156e976400fabe2fd52897cbd9bad2848b93" "reviewed_field" "7b1f85c62fa8e30ce32924c00d6ea8a44bbe32b4b4d91288fa778e14b11f4aa5" "raw_widget" "80e4ede06b701dc7b35441b6287b88eb153c002e3ee5eaf2b81ba074f0f37a59"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-38719-before.png" "sha256" "e7ec2f64b18a86fd897bc39b6d21875447305a7177c662bb691e66cbd8cdac10"} {"path" "q-38719-before.json" "sha256" "1343b37b7ef5ad899524287b5dfa4c1cfc9e4f837bd840ab1361b997d3ba7f28"} {"path" "history-q-38719.png" "sha256" "2f04d20d3d2e272aeb9be5aaa51c8128b225f7daa7fd1690107dcaaead6d87f3"} {"path" "q-38719-after.json" "sha256" "f1246bb6a9752c8189596579d7064133fe01bf9e0ed3f60a2c0d5dea0b64fd69"}]} {"question" "q-38719" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}-y \\\\ y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y\\in R}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,x,z\\in R}"] ["math" "{[\\begin{aligned}y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071281/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "4c76565f6732780519e7e7169fb8dd76e59e0c1fc2b7f627cb2db0b162cd8841" "rationale" "For the original transformation, solving T(x,y,z)=0 gives equations 2x-4y+2z=0, x-3y+z=0, and 4x-4y+z=0. Subtracting twice the second equation from the first gives 2y=0. Subtracting four times the second equation from the third gives 8y-3z=0, hence z=0 and then x=0. Therefore Ker(T)={(0,0,0)}, original option b (index 1). This derived judgment agrees with the authentic worked solution and saved Correct grading. The stored bmatrix and captured bracketed aligned vector preserve the same singleton set and ordered entries."}] "usage" {"e-5439" [] "e-5441" [] "e-5616" [] "e-5440" [] "q-37842" [] "q-37825" [] "q-38676" [] "q-38719" []} "basis" 2540 "retractions" [[17592186125020 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x - 2y \\\\ -10x + 5y\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $u = \\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$. Find the value of $k$ such that $u ∈ Ker(T)$."] [17592186125023 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-8x + 4y \\\\ 6x - 3y \\\\ 2x - y\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $u = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ k \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$. Find the value of $k$ such that $u ∈ Ker(T)$."] [17592186125054 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x - 4y + 2z \\\\ x - 3y + z \\\\ 4x - 4y + z\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $Ker(T)$."] [17592186125054 :question/answer-fields 17592186123346] [17592186125051 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}5x - 7y \\\\ 14y - 10x\\end{bmatrix}$,\n\nfind $3 \\cdot nullity(T) + 1$."] [17592186124273 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$?"] [17592186124273 :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(v_{1}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{2}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{3}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1$."] [17592186124245 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})$."] [17592186124245 :question/worked-solution "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(u) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot k + 12 \\cdot 2 - 3 \\cdot 6 \\\\ -2 \\cdot k + 4 \\cdot 2 - 2 \\cdot 6 \\\\ 6 \\cdot k - 3 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3k + 6 \\\\ -2k - 4 \\\\ 6k + 12\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{3k + 6 = 0; - 2k - 4 = 0; 6k + 12 = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2$."] [17592186124243 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})$."] [17592186124243 :question/worked-solution "Finding the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSetting $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ we then need to solve the equation\n\n$$\nT\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nFirst, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-\\frac{3}{2})R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y$. That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=-2y$.\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124244 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})$."] [17592186124244 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))\n$$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))$, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123143 17592186123144 17592186123145 17592186123146 17592186123147 17592186123161 17592186123162 17592186123163 17592186123164 17592186123165 17592186123329 17592186123330 17592186123331 17592186123332 17592186123333 17592186123347 17592186123348 17592186123349 17592186123350 17592186123351] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123142 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123142 :answer-field/id] [17592186123142 :answer-field/key] [17592186123142 :answer-field/type] [17592186123160 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123160 :answer-field/id] [17592186123160 :answer-field/key] [17592186123160 :answer-field/type] [17592186123328 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123328 :answer-field/id] [17592186123328 :answer-field/key] [17592186123328 :answer-field/type] [17592186123346 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123346 :answer-field/id] [17592186123346 :answer-field/key] [17592186123346 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
